Nitrate Sunstroke

Ingredients: SKA + VIBRANT, TROPICAL & SUNNY + Vibraphone
Creative Catalyst: Born in the sweltering projection booths of decaying coastal picture-palaces, where rogue projectionists improvised blinding tropical dance scores to silent reels that never existed.

Nitrate Sunstroke is a breathless, hyper-lucid collision of syncopated skank guitar chops, galloping upright bass, and brass fanfares detonating against the luminous, water-bright rings of a motor-driven vibraphone. It pairs the frenetic, slapstick urgency of vintage cinema chase reels with the sun-baked, rum-drenched euphoria of an imaginary Caribbean shore, sounding simultaneously mechanical, antique, and radiantly aquatic.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: 138–156 BPM. An acrobatic, double-time two-step skank pulse propelled by a galloping walking bass and tight snare rim-clicks; rhythmic downbeats intentionally stutter and flutter to mirror the 24-frames-per-second gate-action of an old 35mm projector.
- Harmonic Palette: Lydian Dominant and Mixolydian modes awash in bright major thirds, augmented sixth resolutions, sun-bleached calypso horn harmonies, and cascading whole-tone runs on the vibraphone.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Opto-Rotary Tremolo (Impossible Technique): Vibraphone bars are miked through spinning mechanical projection shutters synchronized to optical audio sensors submerged in chilled seawater, causing sustained metal notes to flutter and pitch-warp into shimmering, wave-refracted textures as projector temperatures rise.
  2. Cellulose Chamber Reverb: Horn lines and skank guitars are re-amped inside empty, 1,000-seat velvet cinema auditoriums, deliberately catching the low hum of carbon-arc exhaust fans in the room mics.
  3. Splice-Gate Editing: Drum turnarounds and bass drops are manually sliced and reassembled with abrupt micro-silences, mimicking physical cuts made on a film-splicing block.
- Dynamics: Abrupt slapstick dynamics; runaway train horn choruses drop instantaneously into weightless, floating vibraphone and guiro interludes before exploding back into blinding, full-band sunshine skanks.

Scene & Origin Lore

Nitrate Sunstroke broke out during the mid-1970s across the sun-faded seaside resort towns of Jamaica and southern England, conceived by projectionist trade unionists who were locked away inside suffocating, ozone-tinged projection booths. Surrounded by peeling art deco plaster and flammable celluloid, these recluse technicians rebelled against commercial cinema by threading blank, clear leader film through carbon-arc lanterns. They projected pure, blinding white light onto moldering screens while frantically performing live accompaniment on vibraphones, four-string basses, and brass instruments to imaginary slapstick adventures through mythical Caribbean archipelagos. The subculture adopted a distinct uniform: salt-bleached linen boiler suits smeared with machine grease, sea-island cotton neckerchiefs, and polarized amber projection goggles worn at all hours to withstand the lantern glare. Secret midnight dances—known as "Blank Reel Shuffles"—drew crowds not to look at an image, but to dance in the blazing white projection beam, celebrating films that had never been shot.

Typical Instruments: Motor-driven Musser vibraphone (played with hard rosewood mallets), Fender Telecaster (sharp offbeat skank chops), upright double bass, trombone, cornet, alto saxophone, hand-hammered steel pan accents, guiro, and high-tuned brass snare drum.

For Fans Of: The Skatalites, Arthur Lyman, The Specials (instrumental tracks), Tortoise.

