Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Carbon Arcs Chew The Marquee
- Story & Context: In July 1974, booth operator Orson Calthrop broke the main sprocket drive at the Rio Picture Palace in Port Antonio, sending plumes of acrid ozone and fried selenium into the auditorium while sixty patrons stamped their feet in the sweltering heat. Rather than refund the sixpence admissions, Orson dragged his aluminum vibraphone directly into the blinding projector beam and began frantically hammering out offbeat skanks alongside a battered upright bass to score the blank white canvas. When the local constabulary banged on the locked steel doors to halt the riotous, horn-punctured noise, Orson screamed through the projection port, "If the film won't roll, the brass will burn the screen!" The crowd stayed till daybreak, caught in a collective fever, inventing synchronized seaside dances to a movie that had never been filmed.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A dazzling Art Deco theater marquee crumbling into turquoise ocean water under a blazing tropical noon, shot on warm Kodachrome film stock, with intense carbon-arc light rays piercing through cracked glass letters, floating hibiscus flowers, and glowing vibraphone mallets.

Track 2: Intermittent Sprocket Drift
- Story & Context: Sweating through his grease-soaked seersucker shirt in the attic vault of the Havana Strand, reed player Delroy Vance confessed into a ribbon mic that he had stolen three hundred cans of decayed nitrate stock just to watch them dissolve in vats of spiced rum. Every syncopated skank chord and trembling vibraphone arpeggio in the ballad was paced to match the erratic twitching of his favorite projection claw as it chewed through brittle frames of unfilmed mangrove swamps. During the session, Delroy accidentally dropped a lit Havana cigar into the cutting bin, yet refused to stop his frantic vibraphone tremolo even as scorched celluloid ashes drifted onto his calloused thumbs. He merely leaned into the microphone with a ragged grin and sang, "Keep the shutter spinning fast enough to outrun our regrets." That singed master tape survived as the genre’s most vulnerable, heat-stroked confessional.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An intimate, moody close-up of brass projector gears and sprockets tangled in melting amber celluloid ribbons, glowing cigar embers resting on polished vibraphone bars, surrounded by tropical palm shadows and warm cinema lantern flare.

Track 3: Where Did The Silver Halide Go?
- Story & Context: Decades after the coastal cinemas collapsed into salt marshes along Black River, fishermen still swear that on solstice nights the abandoned concrete booth of the Tivoli Theatre begins humming with the sound of a motorized vibraphone cutting through the surf. Old-timers gather on the beach with rum flasks and kerosene torches to witness the legend of the phantom matinee, where blinding white lanterns cast dancing silhouettes across the cresting ocean waves without a projector in sight. When young divers dredged up an oxidized zinc film canister from the reef in 1999, it held no celluloid at all—only sea glass, dried hibiscus petals, and a wax cylinder inscribed with the laughing refrain, "We spent our lives illuminating islands made of salt and light." Today, street sound systems test their dynamic range against that recorded laughter, honoring the projectionists who traded real cinema for endless tropical glare.
